{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And on the beginnings of your months.</b> It does not say 'and on the head (ראש).' Were it so, I would understand it as the first of the month of Nisan, which is called ראש חדשים (Exodus 12:2). Therefore it says ובראשי חדשיכם — 'and on the beginnings of your months,' meaning: for every single month you shall bring offerings throughout the months of the year. Furthermore, every month has two 'beginnings': in the evening the moon is sighted, and in the morning they would offer the sacrifice. And the new-moon offering was not at night, at the time when the moon is sighted, for no offering is brought at night except the Passover offering; and they would offer it after the daily offering (תמיד)." "<b>Two young bulls, one ram, seven yearling lambs without blemish.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory interpreted: the bulls correspond to Abraham, as it is written (Genesis 18:7): ״ואל הבקר רץ אברהם״; the one ram corresponds to Isaac, as it is written (Genesis 22:13): ״והנה איל אחר נאחז בסבך״; the lambs correspond to Jacob, as it is written (Genesis 30:40): ״והכשבים הפריד יעקב״; and one goat for a sin-offering corresponds to the tribes, as it is written (Genesis 37:31): ״וישחטו שעיר עזים״." "<b>And it is fitting</b> to be roused to ask about the fact that they were eleven — two bulls, one ram, seven lambs, and one goat — behold, eleven offerings that they would bring on the new moon. This corresponds to the eleven hours by which the sun is greater than the moon: for the sun was created on the fourth day and the moon on that same day after eleven hours; and it corresponds to the eleven days by which the days of the sun exceed the days of the moon, for the days of the sun are שס\"ה (365) and the days of the moon are שנ\"ד (354). And the reason they would bring bulls corresponding to Abraham is that he was like two persons — a tumtum and an androginos and a male: Abram — Abraham. And the reason they would bring a ram corresponding to Isaac is that he was the unique one of the flock, a ram as though from the six days of Creation. Seven lambs correspond to Jacob, because his image is engraved on the Throne of Glory, above the seven heavens. And three tenth-ephahs of fine flour, meal-offering mixed with oil, for each bull — corresponding to the three times that ״וירא ה׳ אל אברהם״ is written of him. And two tenth-ephahs of fine flour mixed with oil for the one ram — corresponding to the two times that ״וירא ה׳ אל יצחק״ is written. And 'a tenth-ephah, a tenth-ephah for each lamb' — it should have said 'two tenth-ephahs,' but it says 'a tenth-ephah' corresponding to the one time that is written of him (Genesis 35:9): ״וירא אלהים אל יעקב״; and the reason it doubled and said ״עשרון עשרון״ corresponds to the two names that he has — Jacob and Israel; and furthermore it corresponds to the two tenths of blessings that he received: ten blessings when he was called Jacob and ten blessings when he was called Israel; and thus it is written of him (Genesis 28:22): ״עשר אעשרנו לך״ — this is Levi his son, the tenth among the sons, and the portion of Levi is the tithe (מעשר), and the Levite gives to the priest a tithe of the tithe. And the prophet said (Jeremiah 31:8): ״ואפרים בכורי הוא״; from Ephraim upward to Levi there are ten — behold, a tithe: Gershon, Kehat, and Merari, to the sons of Shimi, Amram, Yitzhar, Hevron, and Uzziel, Aharon — behold, ten. Therefore it is written (I Chronicles 23:13): ״ויבדל אהרן להקדישו קדש קדשים״."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {2 "In the passage about the Levite genealogy, the text appears to list names from later Chronicler sources mixed with sons of Levi; the phrase 'לבני שמעי' may mean 'to the sons of Shimi' as part of the enumeration toward ten. The structure is ambiguous in the Hebrew; translated hyper-literally without resolving the count."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:36:55.439553307Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}